WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
WESLEY CHUN: Well, welcome
back to "Serverless Migration

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.050
Station," where we
show Google Cloud

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.880
users one specific
migration they

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.890
can take to
modernize their apps.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.320
This is the video for
module 1, focusing

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.620
on helping App Engine users to
switch from webapp2 to Flask,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.350
a popular web framework
in the Python community.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.870
Doing so opens the door to
additional modernization

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:19.720
techniques.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:20.220
Hi.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.650
I'm Wesley from Google here
to lead your journey, joined

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.990
by my colleague, Martin, and
our little friend, Porter,

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.150
who will take you from
point A to point B.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.192
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Wes.

00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:28.890
Happy to help.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.800
But why are we
focusing on webapp2?

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.307
I thought App Engine
supported many web frameworks.

00:00:34.307 --> 00:00:36.390
WESLEY CHUN: Yes, that is
absolutely true, Martin.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:39.150
While users can use other
frameworks with App Engine,

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.490
webapp2 comes bundled
with App Engine,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.770
making it the default
choice for many developers,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.650
and unfortunately, not
supported in Python 3.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.410
By the way, if you've been using
a framework that's not webapp,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.990
you may actually
be in better shape.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.270
App Engine is now a
more flexible platform.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:57.180
So moving away from bundled
services, like webapp2,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.280
will make users'
apps more portable.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.340
Also, Flask has more users,
it has more resources,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.260
and is better supported.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.640
Flask isn't the only option.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.710
And generally, any
WSGI-compliant framework

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.600
that does routing is supported.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.220
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, so I get
it for the web framework.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.293
But what about all those App
Engine bundled service APIs?

00:01:15.293 --> 00:01:17.460
WESLEY CHUN: Well, have you
heard of Cloud Datastore

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.930
and Cloud Tasks?

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:22.170
Well, they started out as
App Engine Datastore and Task

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:22.740
Queues.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.720
And since then, they've
matured to becoming

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.520
their own standalone
cloud products

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.850
where you'll find continuing
new features and innovation.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.890
We'll tackle those in
other migration modules,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.427
but this one focuses
just on Flask.

00:01:35.427 --> 00:01:36.510
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:39.900
You said we're going to
migrate a sample app to Flask.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.712
What app?

00:01:40.712 --> 00:01:42.420
WESLEY CHUN: Well, in
the module 0 video,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.100
we gave an overview
of the baseline

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.390
app we're starting from.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.560
So check it out
if you missed it.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.250
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I'd like
to follow what you're doing

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.140
to the code on my own computer.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.830
Can you point me
to the source code?

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.163
WESLEY CHUN: Sure thing, Martin.

00:01:55.163 --> 00:01:55.980
Here's the link.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.470
And by the way, the repo
is maintained regularly,

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:01.270
unlike videos, so the
code may be newer there.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.630
Let's pause so all of
you can go clone the repo

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.290
or download the zip file.

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:07.070
Got it?

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:07.610
Great.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.470
Let's go to the
computer now and start

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:15.810
migrating that module 0 code and
turn it into the module 1 code.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:17.400
Let's start from a good place.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.617
Grab the module 0 code from the
repo and deploy to App Engine.

00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:22.200
If you've been around,
you're probably

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.630
expecting to do
an appcfg update.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:27.480
There's a newer and more
powerful CLI called gcloud

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.430
that comes with the Cloud SDK.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.740
It gives you access
to most Cloud tools,

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.100
instead of just one.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:36.660
So to upload to App Engine,
use gcloud app deploy.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.430
What doesn't change
is that your app

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.100
should be deployed and available
around the world in less

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:41.850
than a minute.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.650
Starting with the
application file,

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.330
main.py, swap the Flask
imports for webapp2s.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.340
Next, while webapp2
is initialized

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.110
at the bottom with
its routing table,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.540
you can do it at
the top with Flask.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.300
Your app is more complex,
but these basic changes

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.830
apply regardless.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.020
Next, this app
only has one route,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.910
which goes to MainHandler.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:05.320
Since we already know this,
you can wipe the table.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.460
But when migrating your app,
don't delete it quite yet.

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.110
You'll probably have
many more routes,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:11.170
which you need to track.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.490
So just comment it out for now
once you get to this point.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.190
There's no change to the
way we access Datastore.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.360
The data model and accompanying
read/write calls stay as-is.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.630
The handler represents the
majority of the affected code.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:29.010
webapp2 uses the handler classes
and corresponding HTTP methods.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.370
In Flask, a handler is just
a route-decorated function,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.600
like this.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.850
Finally, both frameworks render
the template in a similar way,

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.630
so you just switch
to how Flask does it.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:43.260
With that, you're done
migrating the main application.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:46.440
For this migration,
app.yaml stays the same.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.370
The index.html template file
is identical to the original

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.740
with one minor, but
notable, difference.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.740
The Django-style templates
that webapp2 uses automatically

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:58.710
executes callables, whereas
the Jinja2 templating

00:03:58.710 --> 00:04:00.660
system that Flask
uses by default

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.960
requires users to
explicitly add parentheses.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.770
The datetime.ctime() call turns
timestamps into human-readable

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.710
strings, and that's used in
this app to show when visits

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:11.940
occurred.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:15.570
Flask also organizes web
templates in a templates folder

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:20.100
by default, so create it and
move index.html into it before

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.650
or after your edit
to the ctime() call.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.110
Since Flask isn't
bundled like webapp2,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.080
you must add it to a list
of required packages.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.270
The base [INAUDIBLE]
app didn't have any, so

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.400
create a brand new
requirements.txt

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.070
file with just Flask.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.470
If you're unfamiliar with
the requirements.txt file,

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.180
check out its documentation.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.120
For us, the current
version is 1.12.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.520
For a simple app, you probably
don't need the version.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.470
But in any case, the
repo is maintained

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.120
while this video
isn't, meaning you

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.770
may see a newer version there.

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:53.320
First-gen App Engine
runtimes required developers

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.900
to explicitly bundle
third-party packages.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.240
For our app, it's Flask
and its dependencies.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.480
So run this pip install
command to install them

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.480
to the lib folder before
you test or deploy.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.910
A side effect of bundling
third-party packages

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.770
is that you need to tell App
Engine where to find them.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.130
So that's what
appengine_config.py is

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.220
for, to tell the
system to look in lib.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.100
For your apps, you'll create
this file in the same folder

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.930
as your application
code, just like what

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:22.210
we've done with this sample.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:25.950
The only other files in the repo
are a README and .gcloudignore,

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.240
neither of which are critical.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.720
But .gcloudignore is useful in
preventing unnecessary files

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:31.710
to be uploaded.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:34.112
And that's it.

00:05:34.112 --> 00:05:36.070
MARTIN OMANDER: Hey, Wes,
thanks for showing us

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:37.450
how to move to Flask.

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:38.860
Watching this video is great.

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.590
But is there a way for me to
do that migration by hand,

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.750
on my own time,
on that sample app

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.073
before migrating my own code?

00:05:46.073 --> 00:05:47.990
WESLEY CHUN: Well, I'm
glad you asked, Martin.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:48.520
Yes.

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.250
In addition to this video, we
have a corresponding Google

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.000
codelab.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:55.540
Codelabs are free, online,
self-paced, hands-on tutorials,

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.630
so you can do this same
migration on your own time.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.150
The repo links you to
the codelab, the starting

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.163
module 0 code, as well as
the finished module 1 code.

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:05.830
If you get stuck, you
can always go back

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.810
to module 0 or
compare your solution

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.960
to module 1 to see where you
may have gotten off-course.

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:12.670
For your convenience,
here are the docs

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:15.700
to both frameworks so that
you can compare, and contrast,

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:18.873
and better understand the
differences between them.

00:06:18.873 --> 00:06:20.540
MARTIN OMANDER: Now
that you've shown us

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.660
how to unlink part of our
app from dedicated App Engine

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:27.290
libraries, like webapp2
framework, what's next, Wes?

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:30.020
WESLEY CHUN: Well the module
0 app used both webapp2

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.510
and the NDB library
to talk to Datastore.

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:34.430
Now that we're done
with the framework part,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.640
we're going to be focused on
moving from NDB to Cloud NDB,

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.590
so be sure to join us for that.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:42.770
This is Wesley from Google Cloud
on behalf of Martin and Porter.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.470
Thanks for tuning in today, and
we look forward to seeing you

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.420
at the next "Serverless
Migration Station,"

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:51.010
as well as on other
serverless expeditions.

